---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "plasma-projector-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section id="projector-recoil">
    <h2>Stopped · study failed</h2>
    <p>
      Closed 13 November 2026 on request. This study failed to establish how the
      Plasma Projector should feel and look in the game. No final weapon design,
      artwork or implementation scope was accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The assessment: theoretical vector diagrams and numerical questions took
      precedence before the in-game experience was established. Later revisions
      and repeated discussion did not advance the design question. No cause was
      established. This is a failed study, not a successful conclusion inferred
      from its intermediate decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Section 01 is preserved unchanged as the starting premise for a fresh
      study elsewhere. A future study must establish the in-game experience
      first; this closure does not start one or carry provisional mechanics into
      it as mandatory constraints.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Commit to the approach, survive the escape</h2>
    <p>
      A pilot drives towards a derelict or a Gauntlet pirate, gets dangerously
      close and releases enough fire to devastate it. The Plasma Projector is a
      shotgun in role: immense damage nearby, a weaker wash farther out, then a
      vulnerable escape. Its high energy cost and spent interval must make that
      approach a commitment. A recovery as long as ten seconds is worth
      exploring, not a settled cooldown.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plasma engine already gathers light before releasing turbulent fire;
      that family character is the reference. The projector should erupt as one
      broad, brief volume in front of the ship. Ion's precise instant ray
      remains distinct, and another travelling bullet is outside this brief. The
      central tension is overwhelming close-range power against the danger of
      reaching the target and leaving with enough energy to fly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The weapon's close-range role and plasma character are fixed intent; its
      fitting, discharge shape, timing and values remain open. Existing engine
      behaviour, other weapons and current ship-motion laws, including gravity
      and friction, stay fixed. This question concerns one new fitting, not a
      plasma resource system, star redesign or release plan. Readable arcade
      behaviour takes priority over a physical plasma model.
    </p>
    <h3>What the burst needs to resolve</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Preparation and trigger.</strong>
        Does proximity start the glow, with a tighter range triggering automatic
        discharge? Can it hold a charge, and what happens when the target leaves
        or power runs short? Exact release timing remains open within automatic
        firing; no new firing control is proposed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reach and destruction.</strong>
        What breadth, duration and damage falloff reward a close approach
        without making every pass a hit? Can the brief eruption keep consuming
        fragments as a derelict breaks up, rather than spending all its force on
        the first surface? Obstruction and damage through newly exposed material
        need an explicit answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost and recovery.</strong>
        How much energy remains for escape, and how long is the projector
        unavailable afterwards? A long recovery should make the strike
        consequential; its spent state and return to readiness need to be
        readable on the fitting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recoil and approach speed.</strong>
        Could a backwards kick help arrest the approach without automatically
        rescuing a reckless charge? The effect on different ship masses and
        offset mounts remains open.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Form and light.</strong>
        How does the mounted device contain the gathering energy, then release
        turbulent fire without hiding the target, ship or escape route? Charge,
        eruption and recovery must read at flight scale. Derelict destruction
        and a moving pirate are the intended encounter contexts, not claims of
        demonstrated behaviour.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Existing weapon funding rules remain the baseline: per-shot spending, zero
      idle draw, the 10% energy floor and refusal of unaffordable shots. Whether
      charging needs an exception is an explicit design question, not an assumed
      change to those rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      Question test · 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass with findings;
      Grounded — Pass with findings; Consequence — Pass.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/plasma-projector-2026-11-11"
        >Full review and responses</a
      >. Energy and motion boundaries clarified after review; not rerun.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Decisions and observations retained from exploration</h2>
    <p>
      These record what was agreed during this attempt. They do not resolve its
      failed design question or select a production recipe.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A concentrated primary blast with longer, weaker side shoulders was
        preferred. The shoulders vent before the primary and read as cooling
        exhaust from that release, not separate guns. Diagram edges were never
        production geometry; the vector illustrations served an earlier inquiry.
      </li>
      <li>
        Target loss can cancel the primary while venting shoulders with weak
        incidental damage. Preparation covers this cheap residue; the primary
        carries the large release cost and destructive force.
      </li>
      <li>
        Residue-only recovery is quick; primary recovery is long. Check
        full-shot affordability before charging under the existing weapon
        energy-floor rule. This does not reserve energy or guarantee escape
        power.
      </li>
      <li>
        The primary falloff took a middle direction between the alternatives.
        Plasma should devastate the wreck and most fragments caught in its
        powerful region; a conserved global damage budget was rejected.
      </li>
      <li>
        The moving-ship trial supported live LFX at the emitter with persistent
        FX history, and a backward translational kick at primary release. The
        slowing and improved attachment were liked. No angular reversal was
        intended.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/plasma-projector-2026-11-11/structure-reference.svg"
        alt="Schematic short primary framed by two longer shoulders, facing right"
        width="640"
        height="280"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Static structure reference reconstructed at closure from the original
        study paths: 02.2 B primary and 02.3 B shoulders extended 35%. This is
        schematic evidence of the preferred composition, not accepted artwork,
        game scale, damage boundaries or a capture of the retired replay.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>Unresolved</h3>
    <p>
      The actual appearance and feel in the game remain unresolved. The luminous
      replacements and latest comparisons were not accepted. All exact damage,
      costs, timings, reach, recovery, recoil magnitude and mount form remain
      provisional. Stationary and offset-mount recoil, real destruction, moving
      enemies, power loss and escape balance were not established.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reference conditions of the retired forward-flight trial: default Wasp, B1
      course, start (960, −360), heading zero, intact reference derelict held
      100 units ahead. Charge at 1.00 s, shoulders at 1.60 s, primary at 1.68 s;
      staged tick-101 impulse reduced forward velocity from 325 to 255 units/s.
      These were inspection conditions, not accepted tuning. No retained
      recording demonstrates the slowing; that finding relies on feedback and
      the simulation check reported during the session.
    </p>
    <p>
      Temporary study components and painters have been retired. The shared
      flight instrument and its staged-impulse capability remain. Failed visual
      proposals are not retained as references for the next study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / Readiness not reached</h2>
    <p>
      The in-game experience was not established. This study cannot justify
      production delivery.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>04 / No delivery scope</h2>
    <p>Stopped before a supported implementation scope could be defined.</p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
figure img {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
figcaption {
  max-width: 70ch;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
li + li {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
